      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(process): report actual UTF-8 byte count in stdin write message",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where the `process` tool `write` action reported `string.length` (UTF-16 code units) as the byte count for stdin writes, which is incorrect when the written content contains non-ASCII characters.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nProcess stdin data is written as UTF-8. `string.length` counts UTF-16 code units, so CJK characters and emoji underreport the actual byte count. This matches the encoding-aware accounting used by the file-write path.\n\n## User Impact\n\nAgents and users now see accurate byte counts in process stdin write success messages for non-ASCII content.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact head: `5a94e53f485519dfbbb1f408643f6efc52185e7a`\n- AWS Crabbox `cbx_0deb6a105e97`: `pnpm test src/agents/bash-tools.process.input-hints.test.ts` — 6 tests passed\n- Focused regression: `你好😀` is reported as 10 UTF-8 bytes\n- Fresh-PR live PTY proof on AWS Crabbox `cbx_1ff6ae40b733`: real `cat` session returned `Wrote 10 bytes to session [redacted].`\n- `git diff --check` — clean\n- Autoreview against `origin/main` — no accepted/actionable findings\n",
        "branch": "fix/process-write-byte-report",
